being a mom and having
gone through a divorce

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:40.140
and making it up

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.490
in my mind

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:46.170
that I had to raise happy, healthy people.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.890
When I saw my children cheer for me

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:50.820
in that moment,

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.430
and I could hear them from the stage,

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:54.263
I was like,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:58.950
I could, I believed in my mind.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.540
I said, "I did it.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.710
Thank you God.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.810
Thank you mom and dad for the support.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:04.643
Thank you.

00:09:04.643 --> 00:09:05.703
I did it."

00:09:05.703 --> 00:09:07.123
- I'mma cry again.

00:09:07.123 --> 00:09:09.118
- I tell her all the time.

00:09:09.118 --> 00:09:09.951
'Cause again,

00:09:09.951 --> 00:09:11.760
the four kids and also
went through the divorce.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.650
We have share a lot of things in common

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.060
in terms of struggles

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.980
after a divorce, like many women.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.360
And I tell her all the time

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:19.560
when I watch those kids

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:20.850
and how they are with her,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.317
I'll just sit there in awe and say,

00:09:23.317 --> 00:09:25.500
"Your children with you are so,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.270
they're so proud.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.220
They are such good people."
- [Kelly] Beautiful.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:30.720
- She has raised amazing people,

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.930
and I'm very proud of my kids.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.798
But I just hope that the
twins one day like recognize,

00:09:34.798 --> 00:09:36.736
I'm like, you know, I'm on TV.

00:09:36.736 --> 00:09:37.569
- They do.

00:09:37.569 --> 00:09:38.402
- I got a show on.
- They're just,

00:09:38.402 --> 00:09:39.235
they're like, that's great for you.

00:09:39.235 --> 00:09:40.730
- The older two, yeah, but...

00:09:41.786 --> 00:09:42.619
Like, "I'm on Discord."

00:09:42.619 --> 00:09:45.180
(studio laughing)

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.013
- [Lisa] They don't care.

00:09:46.013 --> 00:09:47.550
- I'm like, America loves
this show, by the way.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:51.240
But her kids always, always
supportive and beautiful.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.073
- They better.

00:09:52.073 --> 00:09:53.417
You know what I went through to have them?

00:09:53.417 --> 00:09:55.550
(studio laughing)

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.556
- You better support me.

00:09:57.556 --> 00:09:58.410
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:09:59.970
- Tyler James Williams was here.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:00.840
Another cast member.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.850
And he said Quinta used things

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.710
from his personal life.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.560
All of a sudden, they
would appear in an episode.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.660
And I love it 'cause

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:11.280
like I'm a writer too.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.480
Be careful who you date.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.580
Like, I'm just saying.

00:10:14.498 --> 00:10:15.725
- I'm dating. Hey.
- That's a threat.

00:10:15.725 --> 00:10:16.950
- Does she, does she-
- I'm just saying.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.240
- No. You can't talk yet.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.160
Wait, does she do that to y'all too?

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:21.990
- Absolutely. She will.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.510
I made one mistake.
- Hold on.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.343
- What? What?

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.330
- Look, can I say something?

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.421
- Yes, sure.
- Okay.

00:10:29.421 --> 00:10:30.630
- What I would like to say is

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.120
Tyler was a little,

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.370
he was half right, half wrong.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.690
Tyler says,

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.910
Tyler happens to be similar to Gregory

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:39.990
in some ways.
- [Sheryl] Yes.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:41.010
- That's the truth.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.900
He just happens to be similar to him.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:43.733
Tyler is very,

00:10:43.733 --> 00:10:44.880
he has his paths.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.500
He has the way he sees things,

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.120
and he has the way he likes things done.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:49.260
Now the Sheryl thing,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:50.280
I just wanna say

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.560
what she's gonna say is 100% true.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.440
So I just wanna set that up
that Tyler was half wrong.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.340
Sheryl's completely right.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:57.173
So go ahead.

00:10:57.173 --> 00:10:58.200
- What did she do to you?

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.330
- Okay, so I just wanna say

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.300
when you hear the name Darren Star

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.350
tell me you don't think

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.630
of a six foot basketball player.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.900
Now you know Darren Star
sounds like a black man.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:10.733
Thank you.

00:11:10.733 --> 00:11:12.480
I got it wrong.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.390
Now you know Orlando Bloom.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:16.620
- Yes.
- Tell me you don't think

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.250
about a nice bruising
football player, black man.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.920
Okay, so I got it wrong.
- [Lisa] Definitely the wrong-

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.795
- It was the wrong Orlando.
- [Lisa] Orlando, right.

00:11:25.795 --> 00:11:27.248
- And it was funny.

00:11:27.248 --> 00:11:28.512
- [Kelly] And it was funny.

00:11:28.512 --> 00:11:29.790
- And it was funny.
- She ends up

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:31.977
writing a whole-
- [Lisa] It's so funny.

00:11:31.977 --> 00:11:32.966
- Episode.

00:11:32.966 --> 00:11:34.509
- Cold open.

00:11:34.509 --> 00:11:36.046
- It's so funny.

00:11:36.046 --> 00:11:37.092
- It was hilarious.

00:11:37.092 --> 00:11:38.061
- It was hilarious.

00:11:38.061 --> 00:11:38.894
- [Lisa] It was hilarious.

00:11:38.894 --> 00:11:40.743
- Oh, look at that
Little Carrie Underwood.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:42.810
- Little Carrie Underwood.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.001
That was so good.

00:11:44.001 --> 00:11:45.000
- It was really funny to us in the moment

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.950
because it was me in my room.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.140
We didn't understand the
logic of how Sheryl saw it.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:49.973
We were like, so wait,

00:11:49.973 --> 00:11:51.900
is it black actors who
she thinks are white

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.400
or is it just a name mix up?

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.750
And I was like, what if it's everything?

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.730
And we can get that to Barbara,

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.190
and it'll be good.
- [Kelly] God.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.450
- Everybody seemed to love it.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.350
So anyway.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:04.320
- It's so funny.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.020
- But the day that she opened
the script and read it,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.550
the day of the table read,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.207
she literally went like this:

00:12:10.207 --> 00:12:11.481
"Quinta."

00:12:11.481 --> 00:12:12.314
- She did.

00:12:12.314 --> 00:12:13.788
She like stopped reading for a second.

00:12:13.788 --> 00:12:14.621
I was like, I had to.

00:12:14.621 --> 00:12:15.830
I don't know what to say.

00:12:16.771 --> 00:12:18.060
- You can't now, in Quinta's defense,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:18.893
you can't offer up

00:12:18.893 --> 00:12:19.830
that kind of material
- [Sheryl] No.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.240
- And just have it wasted.
- [Sheryl] It was brilliant.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.530
- That is amazing.

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.783
- Neil Sedaka sounds like a white woman.

00:12:26.041 --> 00:12:26.874
- I've never,

00:12:26.874 --> 00:12:27.707
Who was that?

00:12:27.707 --> 00:12:28.540
- Nevermind.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.561
- Did you just say, "Who is Neil Sedaka?"

00:12:30.561 --> 00:12:31.978
- It was a boomer moment.

00:12:31.978 --> 00:12:32.811
Just let it go.

00:12:32.811 --> 00:12:34.020
- Kelly, do you have anything
you need to move on to?

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.323
They eat me up.

00:12:35.323 --> 00:12:36.945
- I do know Neil Sedaka.

00:12:36.945 --> 00:12:38.040
- Oh, okay.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.170
Y'all know Neil Sedaka?
- I just wanna know what of me

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.335
that you did that it was in my real life.

00:12:42.335 --> 00:12:43.168
- For you?

00:12:43.168 --> 00:12:44.001
- Is there anything?

00:12:44.001 --> 00:12:44.834
Like punching people
- For you?

00:12:44.834 --> 00:12:45.667
- No.
- Okay, good.

00:12:45.667 --> 00:12:47.070
- For you, it went, oh,

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:48.330
that's not out yet.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:51.180
There is an episode coming
up that is very funny

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.280
and very...

00:12:53.397 --> 00:12:54.406
- [Lisa] Have I read it?
- [Kelly] Inspired?

00:12:54.406 --> 00:12:55.239
- No.

00:12:55.239 --> 00:12:56.072
- No, you have not read it yet.

00:12:56.072 --> 00:12:56.905
- Okay, good.

00:12:56.905 --> 00:12:58.151
Well, we'll all be surprised together.

00:12:58.151 --> 00:12:58.984
- It's coming.

00:12:58.984 --> 00:13:00.858
It's coming. Yes, it's coming.

00:13:00.858 --> 00:13:02.553
- I can't wait.
- [Kelly] Love it.

00:13:02.553 --> 00:13:04.380
- Okay, Quinta, so with this show,

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.030
you're also able to give back to teachers.

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:07.710
That's cool.
- [Quinta] Yes, yeah.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:08.660
- It's so exciting.

00:13:10.514 --> 00:13:11.347
We weren't gonna be able to make a show

00:13:11.347 --> 00:13:13.950
about teachers and not help teachers.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.560
It's just too much of
what I consider to be

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.480
a marginalized group and career.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:19.830
While it's one of the oldest,

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:21.829
most continuous professions
- Under appreciated.

00:13:21.829 --> 00:13:23.700
- It still is so underappreciated.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.130
And I guess it's just a me thing

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:27.180
because I think it's crazy

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:28.447
that people are like,

00:13:28.447 --> 00:13:29.820
"Wow, you guys help teachers."

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.100
But I'm like, "How could
I make this show and not?"

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.190
And I'm very fortunate
that both ABC, our network,

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.320
and Warner Brothers, our
studio, were on board.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:38.490
I didn't have to convince them that

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:40.200
that was something we needed to do.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.690
We found many different ways to do it.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:43.860
Our first season,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.530
we had a supply bus that
ran around different cities.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:48.270
We did this Scholastic Book Fair.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:49.410
- I love Scholastic Book Fairs.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:50.850
- [Quinta] Me too.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.350
- And so this year,

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:53.880
we partnered with the water bottle company

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.340
to make sure each kid had
a reusable water bottle.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.143
That was super exciting.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:13:59.993
- That's cool.

00:13:59.993 --> 00:14:00.826
- Really, really cool.

00:14:00.826 --> 00:14:02.910
We're still giving away
supplies like constantly

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:04.920
because teachers just
constantly need supplies.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:05.910
- No, 'cause you know what,

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.110
teachers always have to pay for them.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.360
My mom always had to pay
for a bunch of her stuff.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:10.770
- Well, and then now I feel like people

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.365
go on for wish lists and-
- Well, I was gonna say

00:14:13.365 --> 00:14:14.760
- That's what's good to show-
- Everyone on our staff,

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:18.103
I mean, like Lisa is constantly
putting out DonorsChoose

00:14:18.962 --> 00:14:20.580
and GoFund Me's of the teachers

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:21.930
online who reach out to us.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:22.890
- Well, last summer-
- [Quinta] Carol-

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:23.940
- When we got picked up

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.710
I just paid for everybody's DonorsChoose.

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:27.453
And I went, I'm gonna go broke.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:29.333
- And you will.

00:14:29.333 --> 00:14:30.900
But like that kind of stuff.

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.400
Sheryl, you just helped raise, I think,

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:35.580
what was it, $30,000 for the Texas,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.413
what was the school?

00:14:36.413 --> 00:14:38.020
- Oh, no. Jackson State.
- [Quinta] For Jackson State.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:39.485
- $350,000.

00:14:39.485 --> 00:14:42.245
(audience cheering)

00:14:42.245 --> 00:14:44.730
- Oh my god.
- Excuse me.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.170
- That's like several cars.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:47.003
Not just one.
- Yeah, sorry,

00:14:47.003 --> 00:14:48.420
get in the line, please.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:49.350
No, but it was-

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:50.501
- That's incredible.

00:14:50.501 --> 00:14:51.570
- [All Ladies] Yeah.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.420
- I honestly feel that teachers are

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:58.230
some of the most underprivileged
people on the planet.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.570
- [Kelly] Underappreciated.
- [Lisa] Underappreciated.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:01.620
- And unappreciated.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.980
The mere fact that these
are people who have spent,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.410
who are spending their lives molding

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.590
at the hearts and minds of our future.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.240
They're not babysitters.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.740
They are educators,

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.620
and they deserve much
more than we as people

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:17.760
in our country are giving them.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.620
- Well, you know-
- Much, much more.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:25.560
- Because it was an
historically female profession.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.060
It's part of what we do.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:30.150
We undervalue women in
professions quite often.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.910
It's why nurses are undervalued as well.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.730
So I think that it's an extension of that.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.650
And we need to, you know,

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.080
realize that we've
gotta get more teachers.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:41.079
They have a shortage of teachers-

00:15:41.079 --> 00:15:41.912
- [Sheryl] Absolutely.

00:15:41.912 --> 00:15:42.780
- And part of the reason is

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:43.920
people can't make a living.
- [Kelly] I was gonna say.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.400
- I will never, ever retire.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.550
Don't worry. Thank you.

